   SSttaaggiinngg SSlloopp
     The kernel must parse the firmware memory map tables to know what memory
     it can use.  Since it must allocate memory to do this, llooaaddeerr..eeffii ensures
     there's extra memory available, called “slop”, after everything it loads
     (the kernel, modules and metadata) for the kernel to bootstrap the memory
     allocator.

     By default, amd64 reserves 8MB.  The ssttaaggiinngg__sslloopp command allows for tun‐
     ing the slop size.  It takes a single argument, the size of the slop in
     bytes.

   aammdd6644 NNooccooppyy
     llooaaddeerr..eeffii will load the kernel into memory that is 2MB aligned below
     4GB.  It cannot load to a fixed address because the UEFI firmware may re‐
     serve arbitrary memory for its use at runtime.  Prior to FreeBSD 13.1,
     kernels retained the old BIOS-boot protocol of loading at exactly 2MB.
     Such kernels must be copied from their loaded location to 2MB prior
     starting them up.  The ccooppyy__ssttaaggiinngg command is used to enable this copy‐
     ing for older kernels.  It takes a single argument which can be one of

     _d_i_s_a_b_l_e  Force-disable copying staging area to _2_M.

     _e_n_a_b_l_e   Force-enable copying staging area to _2_M.

     _a_u_t_o     Selects the behaviour based on the kernel's capability of boost‐
              raping from non-2M physical base.  The kernel reports this capa‐
              bility by exporting the symbol _k_e_r_n_p_h_y_s.

     Arm64 loaders have operated in the ‘nocopy’ mode from their inception, so
     there is no ccooppyy__ssttaaggiinngg command on that platform.  Riscv, 32-bit arm and
     arm64 have always loaded at any _2_M_B aligned location, so do not provide
     ccooppyy__ssttaaggiinngg.

           NNoottee.. BIOS loaders on i386 and amd64 put the staging area starting
           at the physical address _2_M, then enable paging with identical map‐
           ping for the low _1_G.  The initial port of llooaaddeerr..eeffii followed the
           same scheme for handing control to the kernel, since it avoided
           modifications for the loader/kernel hand-off protocol, and for the
           kernel page table bootstrap.

           This approach is incompatible with the UEFI specification, and as a
           practical matter, caused troubles on many boards, because UEFI
           firmware is free to use any memory for its own needs.  Applications
           like llooaaddeerr..eeffii must only use memory explicitly allocated using
           boot interfaces.  The original way also potentially destroyed UEFI
           runtime interfaces data.

           Eventually, llooaaddeerr..eeffii and the kernel were improved to avoid this
           problem.

   aammdd6644 FFaauullttss
     Because it executes in x86 protected mode, the amd64 version of
     llooaaddeerr..eeffii is susceptible to CPU faults due to programmer mistakes and
     memory corruption.  To make debugging such faults easier, amd64
     llooaaddeerr..eeffii can provide detailed reporting of the CPU state at the time of
     the fault.

     The ggrraabb__ffaauullttss command installs a handler for faults directly in the
     IDT, avoiding the use of the UEFI debugging interface
     EEFFII__DDEEBBUUGG__SSUUPPPPOORRTT__PPRROOTTOOCCOOLL..RReeggiisstteerrEExxcceeppttiioonnCCaallllbbaacckk().  That interface
     is left available for advanced debuggers in the UEFI environment.  The
     uunnggrraabb__ffaauullttss command tries to deinstall the fault handler, returning TSS
     and IDT CPU tables to their pre-installation state.  The ffaauulltt command
     produces a fault in the llooaaddeerr..eeffii environment for testing purposes, by
     executing the uudd22 processor instruction.

